**Whole task:** take a bare control board and one motor from the box to a live bench rig. It powers on safely, reports its drivers, turns a motor, reads the E-stop and a switch, and stops when you cut the power.

## Parts

- The control board with six TMC5160 drivers, the GX16 power connector and its XT30 lead, the E-stop, one limit switch, and the power button with its JST PH lead. The [bill of materials](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/build/bill-of-materials/) lists each one.
- One NEMA 17 motor, loose on the bench.
- A 24 V DC supply of at least 5 A ([Connecting, "The power supply"](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/04-installation/connecting/)).
- A USB-B cable, a switched power strip within reach, a multimeter, and a computer with a serial terminal.

<TimelapseLinks milestone="m1-bench-it" />

## Concepts

- **The robot system.** The arm, the control board and the PC each have a job. Every 10 ms the PC sends the board a packet ([Robot system](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/01-overview/robot-system/); [Serial protocol](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/board/10-serial-protocol/)).
- **Steppers and drivers.** A motor has 200 full steps per turn. The drivers run at 32 microsteps, so one motor turn is 6,400 microsteps. The motors run open loop, with no encoder ([Drives, ratios and resolution](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/03-technical-data/drives-and-ratios/)).
- **Why the E-stop is normally closed.** Open means stop, so a cut wire or an unplugged button also stops the robot ([Connecting, "The E-stop"](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/04-installation/connecting/)).
- **Why the mains switch matters.** It is the stop that still works when the PC software has frozen ([What you need](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/01-overview/what-you-need/)).

## How-to

Open these official pages at the step that needs them:

- [Stepper drivers](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/board/05-stepper-drivers/): the four ways to destroy the board, including a driver inserted backwards and a driver without its heatsink cemented on.
- [Electronics and wiring](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/build/electronics-and-wiring/) and [Connecting](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/04-installation/connecting/): supply polarity. The GX16 has no fixed +24 V pin, and the board has no reverse-polarity protection.
- [The test firmware](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/board/09-test-firmware/): open a serial terminal that sends a line feed, then use `#IO`, `#LIMIT` and `#STOP`.

## Safety facts that shape the task

The test firmware turns every motor at 1000 steps per second from boot until the power goes off, and no command stops it. `#STOP` only ends a printout ([The test firmware](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/board/09-test-firmware/), danger box).

So in M1 the safe stop is the switched mains strip, and the motor sits loose on the bench. Nothing is mounted in a joint yet.

## Practice

- Meter the supply output and mark + and −.
- Crimp and solder the GX16 plug.
- Check driver orientation by the DIAG pins. Repeat this on every power-on after touching the drivers.
- Drive the serial terminal until the commands are automatic.

## Done check

On camera, from cold:

1. Polarity is metered and marked.
2. The drivers are checked by their DIAG pins.
3. Power goes on through the switched strip.
4. The power-on report shows six `Connection is: GOOD` lines.
5. The motor turns.
6. `#IO` shows about 24000 mV and follows the E-stop as it is pressed and released.
7. `#LIMIT` names the switch while it is pressed.
8. The strip switch stops everything.

This is the build guide's own first check ([Firmware and first run](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/build/firmware-and-first-run/), section 1), with only one motor connected.

**Settled: one motor is enough.** The `GOOD` or `BAD` line is an SPI check of the driver module, and the test firmware's open-load printing is commented out ([`main.cpp`, lines 868 to 936](https://github.com/Source-Robotics/PAROL6-Desktop-robot-arm/blob/e76f48d/PAROL6%20control%20board%20test%20code/src/main.cpp#L868-L936)). Swap motors only with the 24 V off.

At power-up you should hear clicks and see about 1.5 A drawn at 24 V. If not, disconnect ([PAROL6 setup and calibration, from 2:30](https://youtu.be/rv8zt6-3rvo?t=150)).

## Terms

- **TMC5160**: the stepper driver module, in the StepStick format, that the board takes six of. The firmware configures it over SPI ([Stepper drivers](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/board/05-stepper-drivers/)).
- **microstep**: one of the 32 subdivisions of a full step the drivers run at, so one motor turn is 6,400 microsteps ([Drives, ratios and resolution](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/03-technical-data/drives-and-ratios/)).
- **open loop**: control with no position feedback. The motors have no encoders, so the position is the count of steps commanded since homing ([Drives, ratios and resolution](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/03-technical-data/drives-and-ratios/)).
- **normally closed**: a contact that conducts until it is pressed. The E-stop must be one, so pressing it, unplugging it or cutting its wire all stop the robot ([Connecting](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/04-installation/connecting/)).
- **GX16**: the 2-pin circular connector that takes 24 V into the robot's base ([Connecting](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/04-installation/connecting/)).
- **XT30**: the power connector on the control board that the GX16 socket is wired to ([Electronics and wiring](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/build/electronics-and-wiring/)).
- **DIAG pins**: the two pins on a driver module that show its orientation. They must point at the red arrows in the official figure ([Stepper drivers](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/board/05-stepper-drivers/)).
- **test firmware**: the bench firmware every board ships with. It checks the six drivers, turns every motor slowly and reports the inputs over USB ([The test firmware](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/board/09-test-firmware/)).

